// examples/self_host/ssa_lift.fern — lift the self-host STACK IR (ir.fern's
// `Op[]`) into SSA form (ssa.fern's `SFunc`). The self-hosted mirror of
// native's `internal/ssa/lift.go` (`LiftFromIR`): the native compiler lowers
// AST -> ir.Op[] and then LIFTS that stack stream into SSA before its
// optimiser and the `wasm-ssa` backend. Lifting from the IR makes SSA a clean
// DOWNSTREAM layer over the stack IR that is already the default lowering path
// (goal 1), so it inherits the IR's coverage rather than re-deriving it (as
// the parallel AST->SSA `ssa.build_func` does).
//
// SCOPE (slices 0+1):
//   - slice 0: the straight-line integer spine (const_i32, load/store/tee
//     local, the integer binary ops, the `not` unary, call_direct, drop,
//     return) and structured void `if` / `else` / `end` with phi merges.
//   - slice 1: structured `block` / `loop` / `br` / `brif` (BlockTypeVoid),
//     i.e. real LOOPS — the loop header carries a phi per local for every
//     loop-carried variable (entry value vs. back-edge value), exactly as
//     native's lift does, and a `block` exit merges all its `br`/`brif`-out
//     edges. `if`, `block` and `loop` share ONE scope stack so `br`/`brif`
//     relative depths resolve uniformly.
//   - slice 2: i32 ARRAYS — `arr_make` / `arr_get` / `arr_set` / `arr_len`
//     (width 32) lowered to build_func's length-prefixed heap layout
//     (element 0 = length, data at 1..n), plus stripping of irlower's RC
//     runtime-helper calls (`__fern_rc_inc` / `__fern_rc_dec` /
//     `__fern_str_free`) to no-ops — SSA's bump heap never frees, so it
//     inherits build_func's leak-only memory model.
//   - slice 3: scalar-field STRUCTS — `struct_make` / `struct_get` /
//     `struct_set` (width-0 i32 / boolean / pointer fields) lowered to
//     build_func's tag-prefixed layout (slot 0 = tag, field f at slot f+1).
//     Width-64 (f64 / i64) fields bail.
//   - slice 4: TUPLES — `tuple_make` / `tuple_get` / `tuple_set` (scalar /
//     pointer elements) lowered to build_func's ExprTuple layout (element i
//     at slot i, no prefix). Width-64 elements bail.
//   - slice 5: f64 SCALARS — `const_f64_text`, the f64 arithmetic / compare
//     ops (fadd / fsub / fmul / fdiv / feq / … via fbin_sym) and `fneg`,
//     lowered to SSA const_float / binary / unary. Value widths are inferred
//     downstream by ssa.compute_isfloat, so the lift emits no float tag.
//   - slice 6: i32<->f64 CASTS — `i32_to_f64` -> unary "as_f64" (cvtsi2sd),
//     `f64_to_i32` -> unary "as_i32" (cvttsd2si, truncating). f64->i64 stays
//     out (i64 territory).
//   - slice 7: STRING ops — `str_concat` -> the `concat` inst (fresh heap
//     string), `str_eq` -> the `streq` inst (content compare; `!=` is str_eq
//     + not). Both mirror build_func's string `+` / `==` lowering.
//   - slice 8: STRING BUILDER — `strbuf_reset` / `strbuf_append` (void) /
//     `strbuf_take` (pushes the built string) -> the matching SSA strbuf
//     insts, the global amortised-O(1) builder build_func also emits.
//   - slice 9: PROCESS / OUTPUT — `print_str` -> the `write` inst (raw stdout,
//     no newline), `eprint_str` -> the `print` inst fd 2 (stderr + newline),
//     `exit` -> the `exit` inst (a diverging regular inst, not a terminator).
//   - slice 10: STRING INDEX — `str_index` (`s[i]`) -> load_elem at word i+1,
//     the same [len, byte-per-word] shape arr_get uses.
//   - slice 11: OPTION / RESULT — `opt_make` (Some/Ok/Err) / `opt_none` /
//     `opt_tag` / `opt_payload` lowered to build_func's 2-word { tag@0,
//     payload@1 } box (payload 8-byte so pointers round-trip). The i64 / f64
//     payload variants (opt_make_w / opt_payload_w, width 64) bail.
//   - slice 12: `args` -> the SSA `args` inst (the argv string[]).
//   - slice 13: CLOSURES — `const_func` -> the `funcaddr` inst (a function
//     value / lambda code pointer) and `call_indirect` -> the SSA
//     call_indirect (target = the popped code pointer, env-FIRST args). The
//     closure box is an `arr_make` [funcval, caps…] and captures read via
//     `arr_get` (both already lifted); the lifted lambda takes env as param 0.
//   - slice 14: user ENUM matching — `variant_is(name)` -> load_elem(box, 0)
//     `==` the variant's struct id. An enum value is a `struct_make` box whose
//     slot 0 now holds the variant's id (struct_id_of over the module's
//     parser-desugared variant structs), so the tag WRITTEN at construction and
//     the tag TESTED by the match share one mapping and agree by construction.
//     The payload is bound via `struct_get` (already lifted). This needs the
//     module's struct table threaded into `lift_from_ir` (the `structs` param).
//   - slice 15: array APPEND / SLICE — `arr_push` / `arr_push_owned` ->
//     `__ssa_arr_push(arr, val)` and `arr_slice` / `str_slice` ->
//     `__ssa_arr_slice(arr, lo, hi)`, the same pure-Fern runtime helpers
//     build_func's `.append` / `a[lo:hi]` lowerings call. The emit driver
//     injects the called helpers (build_func over ssa.ssa_helpers_src), since
//     they use `__new_array` (a build_func intrinsic irlower never emits) and so
//     can't themselves be lifted. arr_push_owned collapses to the copying push
//     (the bump heap never frees), and str_slice copies a fresh substring rather
//     than the native zero-copy immortal-view box — one leak-only memory model.
//   - slice 16: `str_from_bytes` (string_from_bytes_unchecked(bs)) ->
//     `__ssa_arr_slice(bs, 0, bs.len())`, a full-array copy that IS the string
//     (byte array and string share the layout), reusing the slice-15 helper.
//   - slice 17: i64 — `const_i64` (source text -> movabsq / ldr =), i64
//     arithmetic + comparison (the existing `binary` inst, now width-aware:
//     compute_widths propagates 64-bit through arithmetic operands so the
//     backends pick the q-suffixed / x-register ops and a comparison still
//     yields i32), and the width casts `int_extend` (i32 -> i64 via
//     sext_i64 / zext_i64) and `int_wrap` (i64 -> i32 truncate). i64 locals
//     ride the value slots (already 8-byte) with width flowing through
//     copy / phi. Values that don't fit i32 no longer bail.
// Everything else (value-producing ifs, f64 across calls / composite
// elements, the libm intrinsics, map ops) sets `ok=false` so a caller falls
// back, mirroring how native's lift errors. Validated without a backend by running the lifted
// SFunc through `ssa.eval_func` (the ssa.fern self-validation pattern).
//
// The lift is a single linear pass over the op stream; the structured-control-
// flow scopes are tracked on the explicit stack described above.

import "./ir";
import "./ssa";
import "./util";
import "./parser";

// struct_id_of returns the index of the struct/enum-variant named `name` in the
// module's struct list, or -1 if absent. Both `struct_make` (the tag it stores
// at slot 0) and `variant_is` (the discriminant it compares) use THIS single
// mapping, so an enum box's tag and its match test agree by construction — the
// lift is self-consistent without matching build_func's exact numbering.
function struct_id_of(structs: parser.StructDecl[], name: string): i32 {
    var i: i32 = 0;
    while (i < structs.len()) {
        if (structs[i].name == name) { return i; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// `bail` names the IR op kind that forced ok=false (or "unbalanced-scope"),
// "" when the lift succeeded — a diagnostic for the coverage scan; the
// production drivers read only .func / .ok.
pub struct LResult { func: ssa.SFunc, ok: boolean, bail: string }

// ---- small array helpers (value-semantics, no slice syntax) -------------

function vset(a: i32[], idx: i32, val: i32): i32[] {
    var out: i32[] = [];
    var i: i32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len()) {
        if (i == idx) { out = out.append(val); } else { out = out.append(a[i]); }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}
function drop_last(a: i32[]): i32[] {
    var out: i32[] = [];
    var i: i32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len() - 1) { out = out.append(a[i]); i = i + 1; }
    return out;
}
function drop_last2(a: i32[]): i32[] {
    var out: i32[] = [];
    var i: i32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len() - 2) { out = out.append(a[i]); i = i + 1; }
    return out;
}
// drop_last_s is drop_last for a string[] (the scope-kind stack).
function drop_last_s(a: string[]): string[] {
    var out: string[] = [];
    var i: i32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len() - 1) { out = out.append(a[i]); i = i + 1; }
    return out;
}
// drop_n drops the last n elements (used to pop a stride-nslots scope row).
function drop_n(a: i32[], n: i32): i32[] {
    var out: i32[] = [];
    var i: i32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len() - n) { out = out.append(a[i]); i = i + 1; }
    return out;
}

// ---- IR binary-op kind -> SSA `binary`/`unary` op string ----------------

// is_rc_noop reports whether a call target is an irlower RC runtime helper the
// lift strips (SSA has no free — see the arr_make note). All of them take the
// subject box as their FIRST argument and return it (or void with the result
// drop'd), so substituting that box for the call's result is sound on the
// leak-only SSA heap — including when the result FLOWS onward (the
// consume-rebind `__field_reclaim_<T>(new, old, snap) -> new` is store_local'd).
// has_prefix reports whether `s` begins with `p` (a byte-wise compare — the
// compiler's own checker has no `.starts_with` method).
function has_prefix(s: string, p: string): boolean {
    if (s.len() < p.len()) { return false; }
    var i: i32 = 0;
    while (i < p.len()) {
        if (s[i] != p[i]) { return false; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

function is_rc_noop(name: string): boolean {
    if (name == "__fern_rc_inc") { return true; }
    if (name == "__fern_rc_dec") { return true; }
    // #4873 grow-bracket share helpers (register-backend rc aliases; wasm
    // no-ops) — same take-the-box-return-it shape, always drop'd.
    if (name == "__fern_arr_share_inc") { return true; }
    if (name == "__fern_arr_share_dec") { return true; }
    if (name == "__fern_str_free") { return true; }
    if (name == "__fern_str_arr_free") { return true; }
    // Per-type reclamation helpers irlower emits at scope exit (deep-drop of a
    // struct's rc-tracked fields / a consume-rebind field reclaim). The type
    // name is a dynamic suffix; both take + return the subject box (arg 0), so
    // the box-for-result stand-in is sound on the leak-only SSA heap.
    if (has_prefix(name, "__struct_drop_")) { return true; }
    if (has_prefix(name, "__field_reclaim_")) { return true; }
    return false;
}

function bin_sym(k: string): string {
    if (k == "add") { return "+"; }
    if (k == "sub") { return "-"; }
    if (k == "mul") { return "*"; }
    if (k == "div_s") { return "/"; }
    if (k == "div_u") { return "/"; }
    if (k == "rem_s") { return "%"; }
    if (k == "rem_u") { return "%"; }
    if (k == "eq") { return "=="; }
    if (k == "ne") { return "!="; }
    if (k == "lt_s") { return "<"; }
    if (k == "lt_u") { return "<"; }
    if (k == "le_s") { return "<="; }
    if (k == "le_u") { return "<="; }
    if (k == "gt_s") { return ">"; }
    if (k == "gt_u") { return ">"; }
    if (k == "ge_s") { return ">="; }
    if (k == "ge_u") { return ">="; }
    if (k == "and") { return "&"; }
    if (k == "or") { return "|"; }
    if (k == "xor") { return "^"; }
    if (k == "shl") { return "<<"; }
    if (k == "shr_s") { return ">>"; }
    if (k == "shr_u") { return ">>"; }
    return "";
}

// fbin_sym maps an f64 IR op kind to its SSA `binary` symbol. Arithmetic
// (fadd/…) yields an f64; a compare (feq/…) yields an i32 0/1. The SSA layer
// needs no float tag from the lift: ssa.compute_isfloat classifies each value
// (const_float / f64-arg / float-operand arithmetic) downstream, and each
// backend selects the float instruction (addsd / ucomisd, fadd / fcmp) from
// that — exactly as build_func's float `binary`s are emitted.
function fbin_sym(k: string): string {
    if (k == "fadd") { return "+"; }
    if (k == "fsub") { return "-"; }
    if (k == "fmul") { return "*"; }
    if (k == "fdiv") { return "/"; }
    if (k == "feq") { return "=="; }
    if (k == "fne") { return "!="; }
    if (k == "flt") { return "<"; }
    if (k == "fle") { return "<="; }
    if (k == "fgt") { return ">"; }
    if (k == "fge") { return ">="; }
    return "";
}

// ---- the lift ------------------------------------------------------------

pub function lift_from_ir(name: string, nparams: i32, nslots: i32, ops: ir.Op[], structs: parser.StructDecl[]): LResult {
    var nvals: i32 = 0;
    var next_blk: i32 = 0;
    var bail: string = "";   // the op kind that forced a fall-back (scan diagnostic)

    var blocks: ssa.SBlock[] = [];
    var cur_id: i32 = next_blk; next_blk = next_blk + 1;   // entry = block 0
    var cur_insts: ssa.SInst[] = [];
    var cur_preds: i32[] = [];
    var cur_term: ssa.STerm = STerm_none();
    var cur_dead: boolean = false;     // current block is unreachable (post-branch)

    var stack: i32[] = [];
    var slots: i32[] = [];
    var ok: boolean = true;

    // params -> value ids 0..nparams-1; declared locals -> const_int 0.
    var p: i32 = 0;
    while (p < nparams) {
        var pv: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
        cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 6, result: pv, args: [], imm: p, str: "" });
        slots = slots.append(pv);
        p = p + 1;
    }
    while (slots.len() < nslots) {
        var zv: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
        cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 1, result: zv, args: [], imm: 0, str: "" });
        slots = slots.append(zv);
    }

    // ---- unified scope stack (if / block / loop), innermost = last entry --
    // Per-scope (one entry each): kind, uid (edge ownership), target block id
    // (if->merge, block->exit, loop->header), and the if-only bookkeeping.
    var sc_kind: string[] = [];
    var sc_uid: i32[] = [];
    var sc_target: i32[] = [];
    var sc_else: i32[] = [];
    var sc_seen_else: i32[] = [];
    var sc_then_blk: i32[] = [];
    var sc_then_reaches: i32[] = [];
    var sc_parent: i32[] = [];
    // Per-scope stride-nslots rows (kept for EVERY scope so positions align):
    var sc_snap: i32[] = [];          // if: pre-if slots; else: unused
    var sc_then_slots: i32[] = [];     // if: then-arm exit slots
    var sc_header_phis: i32[] = [];    // loop: header phi value-id per slot
    var next_uid: i32 = 1;

    // ---- branch edges (global, tagged by owner scope uid) ----------------
    // Each br/brif records the predecessor block + its slot vector; the target
    // scope consumes its edges at `end` (loop -> header phis; block -> exit
    // merge). edge_vals is flattened stride-nslots.
    var edge_owner: i32[] = [];
    var edge_block: i32[] = [];
    var edge_vals: i32[] = [];

    var i: i32 = 0;
    while (i < ops.len() && ok) {
        var op: ir.Op = ops[i];
        var k: string = ir.kind_name(op.kind_tag);

        // Skip dead code after a terminator (e.g. ops after a `return`/`br`
        // inside an arm); only else/end/the matching block delimiters act on it.
        if (cur_term.kind_tag != 0 && k != "else" && k != "end") {
            i = i + 1;
            continue;
        }

        if (k == "const_i32") {
            var v: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
            cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 1, result: v, args: [], imm: op.i32_imm, str: "" });
            stack = stack.append(v);
        } else if (k == "const_i32_text") {
            // irlower carries integer literals as their SOURCE TEXT (so the
            // AST backends splice hex / full-u32 values straight into the
            // immediate). The SSA `const_int` holds a parsed i32, so parse the
            // text — but only plain DECIMAL (optionally signed); a hex / non-
            // decimal literal (which util.digits_to_i32 can't parse) bails so a
            // caller falls back rather than emitting a wrong constant.
            var t: string = op.str;
            var dec: boolean = t.len() > 0;
            var ci: i32 = 0;
            while (ci < t.len()) {
                var c: i32 = t[ci] as i32;
                if (ci == 0 && c == 45) {
                    if (t.len() == 1) { dec = false; }
                } else if (c < 48 || c > 57) { dec = false; }
                ci = ci + 1;
            }
            if (!dec) { ok = false; }
            else {
                var ctv: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
                cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 1, result: ctv, args: [], imm: util.digits_to_i32(t), str: "" });
                stack = stack.append(ctv);
            }
        } else if (k == "const_f64") {
            // An f64 literal, carried as source text (its bits don't fit an
            // immediate) → the SSA const_float inst, which each backend
            // materialises in a data section. compute_isfloat seeds float-ness
            // from this inst. Matches build_func's ExprNumber(is_float) path.
            var fcv: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
            cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 4, result: fcv, args: [], imm: 0, str: op.str });
            stack = stack.append(fcv);
        } else if (k == "const_i64") {
            // A 64-bit integer literal, carried as source text (its value may not
            // fit the i32 SInst.imm) → the SSA `const_i64` inst, which each
            // backend splices straight into a full-width immediate (movabsq /
            // ldr =). compute_widths marks the value 64-bit, so i64 arithmetic
            // over it lowers to the q-suffixed / x-register ops. Accept a decimal
            // (optionally signed) or 0x-hex literal — the two forms irlower emits
            // — and bail on anything else so a caller falls back rather than
            // splicing junk into the immediate.
            var it: string = op.str;
            var iok: boolean = it.len() > 0;
            var istart: i32 = 0;
            if (it.len() > 0 && it[0] == 45) { istart = 1; if (it.len() == 1) { iok = false; } }
            var ishex: boolean = it.len() > istart + 1 && it[istart] == 48 && (it[istart + 1] == 120 || it[istart + 1] == 88);
            var idi: i32 = istart;
            if (ishex) { idi = istart + 2; if (it.len() <= idi) { iok = false; } }
            while (idi < it.len()) {
                var ic: i32 = it[idi] as i32;
                var isdec: boolean = ic >= 48 && ic <= 57;
                var ishexd: boolean = ishex && ((ic >= 97 && ic <= 102) || (ic >= 65 && ic <= 70));
                if (!isdec && !ishexd) { iok = false; }
                idi = idi + 1;
            }
            if (!iok) { ok = false; }
            else {
                var icv: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
                cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 3, result: icv, args: [], imm: 0, str: it });
                stack = stack.append(icv);
            }
        } else if (k == "int_cast") {
            // `x as i32` lowers to a sign-extend of the low 32 bits (movslq) —
            // identity for an i32 value, so the SSA value flows through with no
            // inst. The subword / unsigned casts (u8/u32), which
            // actually mask or re-extend, are out of the integer subset.
            if (op.str != "i32") { ok = false; }
        } else if (k == "int_extend") {
            // i32 -> i64 widen (`x as i64` / `as u64`): a unary that sign-extends
            // (movslq / sxtw) a signed source or zero-extends (op.unsigned) an
            // unsigned one. compute_widths marks sext_i64 / zext_i64 results
            // 64-bit, so downstream i64 arithmetic uses the 64-bit ops.
            if (stack.len() < 1) { ok = false; }
            else {
                var iea: i32 = stack[stack.len() - 1];
                stack = drop_last(stack);
                var iestr: string = "sext_i64";
                if (op.unsigned) { iestr = "zext_i64"; }
                var iev: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
                cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 10, result: iev, args: [iea], imm: 0, str: iestr });
                stack = stack.append(iev);
            }
        } else if (k == "int_wrap") {
            // i64 -> i32 truncate: keep the low 32 bits (the value's 32-bit home
            // already holds them). A unary "wrap_i32" whose result compute_widths
            // leaves 32-bit, mirroring build_func's narrowing `as i32`.
            if (stack.len() < 1) { ok = false; }
            else {
                var iwa: i32 = stack[stack.len() - 1];
                stack = drop_last(stack);
                var iwv: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
                cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 10, result: iwv, args: [iwa], imm: 0, str: "wrap_i32" });
                stack = stack.append(iwv);
            }
        } else if (k == "const_str") {
            // A string literal → the SSA const_str inst (materialised as a
            // [len, byte-per-word] block by ssa_x86 / eval_func). The value is
            // a pointer that flows through slots like any other.
            var csv: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
            cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 5, result: csv, args: [], imm: 0, str: op.str });
            stack = stack.append(csv);
        } else if (k == "str_len") {
            // `s.len()` → the length stored at word 0 of the string block, i.e.
            // load_elem(s, 0) — exactly how build_func lowers `.len()`.
            if (stack.len() < 1) { ok = false; }
            else {
                var sp: i32 = stack[stack.len() - 1];
                stack = drop_last(stack);
                var zc: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
                cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 1, result: zc, args: [], imm: 0, str: "" });
                var slv: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
                cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 15, result: slv, args: [sp, zc], imm: 0, str: "" });
                stack = stack.append(slv);
            }
        } else if (k == "str_index") {
            // `s[i]` → the byte at word i+1 (word 0 is the length). The SSA
            // string is a [len, byte-per-word] block — the same shape as an i32
            // array — so this is arr_get's lowering: load_elem at idx+1,
            // mirroring build_func's ExprIndex on a string.
            if (stack.len() < 2) { ok = false; }
            else {
                var sixi: i32 = stack[stack.len() - 1];
                var sixb: i32 = stack[stack.len() - 2];
                stack = drop_last2(stack);
                var sione: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
                cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 1, result: sione, args: [], imm: 1, str: "" });
                var sioff: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
                cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 9, result: sioff, args: [sixi, sione], imm: 0, str: "+" });
                var siv: i32 = nvals; nvals = nvals + 1;
                cur_insts = cur_insts.append(ssa.SInst { kind_tag: 15, result: siv, args: [sixb, sioff], imm: 0, str: "" });
                stack = stack.append(siv);
            }
        } else if (k == "str_concat") {
